function [cellTables, plotTables, tableCounter, GAVsubsTable] = ep_ADF(Y, ANOVAdata, OPT1, ANOVAoptions, OPT2, factorNames, levelNames, factorGroupNames, levelGroupNames, elecFactors, sessFactors, alpha, followup, OPT3, SCALE, LOC1, LOC2, outfid, followupWithin, followupBetween, theHeader, allPostHoc, ANOVAinfo, cellTables, plotTables, tableCounter, lastFlag)
%function [cellTables, plotTables, tableCounter,GAVsubsTable] = ep_ADF(Y, ANOVAdata, OPT1, PER, OPT2, factorNames, levelNames, factorGroupNames, levelGroupNames, elecFactors, sessFactors, alpha, followup, OPT3, SCALE, LOC1, LOC2, outfid, followupWithin, followupBetween, theHeader, allPostHoc, ANOVAinfo, cellTables, plotTables, tableCounter, lastFlag);
%ADF algorithms made available by Harvey Keselman (kesel@Ms.UManitoba.CA)
%Department of Psychology, University of Manitoba, Winnipeg, Canada.
%http://www.umanitoba.ca/cgi-bin/psychology/hpg_main.cgi?data/Keselman.txt
%
% The ADF function is a front end for the WJGLMml function.  It automatically generates the appropriate contrasts
% for both between and within factors.  It also computes the averaged trimmed cell means for each of the contrasts.
% Finally, it presents the full set of tests, along with the appropriate cell means.
% It can present either the robust statistics or the conventional statistics.
% The conventional ANOVA results have been validated against both SAS and SPSS.  One point of difference is that by default SPSS excludes the interactions between covariates
% and between group factors.  This implementation instead follows the SAS default (well, sort of a default) of including them.
% If comparing against SPSS, remember to set the missing data value in SPSS (-999 by default).
% Missing data causes the entire observation to be dropped, even for follow-up ANOVAs and contrasts that only use a subset of the data.
% The trimming and the winsorization is conducted per cell.  The test statistics, means, and variances are then generated by taking a linear combination of the cell numbers as needed.
% This also means that cells are being equally weighted regardless of sample size, so for between group ANOVAs the overall mean may not be the same as that generated by ignoring groups.
%
%Front end programmed by Joseph Dien (jdien07@mac.com)
%
%Keselman, H. J., Wilcox, R. R., & Lix, L. M. (2003). A generally robust approach
%to hypothesis testing in independent and correlated groups designs
%Psychophysiology, 40, 586-596.
%
%Keselman, H. J., Algina, J., Lix, L. M., Wilcox, R. R., & Deering, K. N. (2008).
%A generally robust approach for testing hypotheses and setting confidence intervals for effect sizes.
%Psychological Methods, 13(2), 110.
%
%McCarthy, G., & Wood, C. C. (1985). Scalp distribution of event-related potentials: An ambiguity associated with
%analysis of variance models. Electroencephalography and Clinical Neurophysiology, 62, 203-208.
%
%McGraw, K. O., & Wong, S. P. (1996). Forming inferences about some intraclass correlation coefficients. Psychological Methods, 1(1), 30–46. https://doi.org/10.1037/1082-989X.1.1.30
%
%Qin, S., Nelson, L., McLeod, L., Eremenco, S., & Coons, S. J. (2019). Assessing test–retest reliability of patient-reported outcome measures using intraclass correlation coefficients: Recommendations for selecting and documenting the analytical formula. Quality of Life Research, 28(4), 1029–1033. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11136-018-2076-0
%
%Wilcox, R. R. (2022).  Introduction to Robust Estimation and Hypothesis Testing, 5th Edition.  London: Academic Press.
%Algina, J., Keselman, H. J., & Penfield, R. D. (2005a). An Alternative to Cohen’s Standardized Mean Difference Effect Size: A Robust Parameter and Confidence Interval in the Two Independent Groups Case. Psychological Methods, 10(3), 317–328. https://doi.org/10.1037/1082-989X.10.3.317
%Algina, J., Keselman, H. J., & Penfield, R. D. (2005b). Effect Sizes and their Intervals: The Two-Level Repeated Measures Case. Educational and Psychological Measurement, 65(2), 241–258. https://doi.org/10.1177/0013164404268675
%
% Effect sizes:
% For robust statistics,
% Following guidance from Wilcox (2022), ξ (explanatory measure of effect size) is provided for between-group effects (but not available if more than eight levels are involved), D (positional distance) is provided for within-group effects, and nothing for interactions involving both as they are still under development.
% Furthermore, for pairwise effects, dR (or what he calls AKP).
% For conventional statistics,
% effect sizes are displayed as partial eta squared much like SPSS.  To facilitate use of G*POWER, the f statistic is also provided for F tests.  When using G*POWER, it will be necessary to use the
% options button to specify that the SPSS version of the f statistic is being used (that is, the correlations between the variables is included in the f statistic).
% Lakens (2013) recommendations are used for the t-test effect sizes.  Effect size equations based on spreadsheet in supplemental data.
% Lakens (https://osf.io/ixgcd/) has since retracted his recommendation to use the drm measure if it is closer to d than dav, so this function reflects his updated procedure (as of 1/20/21).
%
% Lakens, D. (2013). Calculating and reporting effect sizes to facilitate cumulative science: A practical primer for t-tests and ANOVAs. Front Psychol, 4, 863. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2013.00863
%
%
%Inputs
%  Y	    : Input data (rows=subjects, columns = cells).  The first NX rows will be assigned to the first group and so forth.
%  ANOVAdata
%    .subjects  : Number of subjects in each group.  Set to empty set if no between group factors.
%    .index     : Original order of the subjects, which have now been sorted according to between groups.
%  OPT1     : Activate trimming option.  0 = no and 1 = yes.
%  ANOVAoptions : preferences for the ANOVA function.
%    .trimming  : Percentage to trim the means.  .05 is the recommended number for ERP data.
%    .bootstrap	: Number of simulations used to generate bootstrapping statistic.  p-values will be unstable if too low. 49999 informally recommended.
%    .reps  : Number of repetitions to determine degree of p-value variability.  Eleven informally recommended.
%    .seed 	: Seed for random number generation.  0 specifies random seed. 1000 arbitrarily suggested as seed to ensure results are replicable.
%    .missing  : Number to be treated as a missing value.  Observations with missing values are dropped from the analysis.
%    .epsilon  : type of epsilon correction for conventional within-group effects ('Greenhouse-Geisser','Huynd-Feldt','Lower Bound').
%    .posthoc  : type of post-hoc for conventional between-group follow-ups ('Tukey','Dunn-Sidak').
%  OPT2 	: Activate Welch-James statistic and bootstrapping.  0 = no and 1 = yes.
%  factorNames: The names of the within factors.  Each should be three letters long.
%               The order should correspond to the structure of the data with the first varying the slowest.
%               Set to empty set if no within factors.
%  levelNames : The names of the levels within each within factor.  One row for each factor.  Each level name should be a single letter.
%               The order should correspond to the order of the levels in the data.
%  factorGroupNames: The names of the between factors.  Each should be three letters long.
%                    The order should correspond to the structure of the data with the first varying the slowest.
%  levelGroupNames : The names of the levels within each between factor.  One row for each factor.  Each level name should be a single letter.
%                    The order should correspond to the order of the levels in the data.
%  elecFactors     : An array indicating which within group factors are electrode factors and hence need the vector
%                    scaling test.  0=not electrode factor, 1=electrode factor.  To disable, just set all to zeroes.
%  sessFactors     : An array indicating which within group factors are session factors and hence need the ICC reliabity output.
%                    0=not session factor, 1=session factor.  To disable, just set all to zeroes.
%  alpha :   The alpha thresholds for significance
%    .uncorrected:  Alpha for a priori interest (.05)
%    .corrected:  Alpha for posthoc multiple comparison corrected.
%  followup: How many levels deep a follow-up this is with zero being not a follow-up.
%  OPT3     : Provide effect sizes. 0 = no and 1 = yes.
%  SCALE    : "SCALE is a scalar indicator to control the use of a scaling factor for the effect size estimator
%             (i.e., .642 for 20% symmetric trimming) when robust estimators are adopted.
%             It takes a value of 0 or 1; a zero indicates that no scaling factor will be used,
%             while a 1 indicates that a scaling factor will be adopted. The default is SCALE=1.
%  LOC1&LOC2: "If the user specifies LOC1 = 0 and LOC2 = 0, the square root of the average of the variances over the cells
%             involved in the contrast is used as the standardizer. If the
%             user specifies LOC1 = 99 and LOC2 = 99, no standardizer is selected."
%  outfid:  The fid of the output file.  Will request output file if not provided.
%  followupWithin: The within group factors being followed up on.
%  followupBetween: The between group factors being followed up on.
%  theHeader: the first seven lines of the ANOVA file plus the number of Bonferroni corrections.
%  allPostHoc: flag to run all posthocs even if effect not significant.
%  ANOVAinfo
%     .betweenLvl: optional cell array of between group labels.  Used for conventional ANOVAs (subs,vars).
%     .covariate: 1 if factor labels and 2 if covariate (vars)
%     .leftColumn: leftmost column of the variables
%     .rightColumn: rightmost column of the variables
%   cellTables: Cell array with the HTML text (SD, SE, CI, N)
%   plotTables: cell array (plots, within cell lablels)
%   tableCounter: Counter for the current html table, label, and plot number.
%   lastFlag: 0 if not the final file and 1 if it is
%Outputs
%   cellTables: Cell array with the HTML text (SD, SE, CI, N)
%   plotTables: cell array (error bar plots, within cell lablels, outlier plots)
%   tableCounter: Counter for the current html table, label, and plot number.
%   GAVsubsTable: Cell array (between levels, within levels) with labels in the first row and column.
%      The levels are all the possible levels of the tested effects (e.g., N,W,R,U,NR,NU,WR,WU).
%      Each cell contains the list of trimmed subjects (original numbering) going into each cell.
%      The first row contains the list of cells going into the cell.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

% bugfix 3/25/08 JD
% Fix for p-value bug where it would output just "0" if there were no zeroes in the decimal.
%
% modified 10/29/08 JD
% Less decimals for DF and test statistic outputs
%
% modified 11/8/08 JD
% When p-value less than .00000001, print out less than sign.  Improved statistics output.
%
% modified 3/25/09 JD
% changed level names to cell array.  Added alpha criteria variable so can highlight results.
% saves output file in html format.
%
% modified 7/24/09 JD
% Added testing of electrode factor effects using the McCarthy & Woods (1985) vector scaling test.
% Automatic follow-up tests to significant interactions
%
% bugfix 11/6/09 JD
% Fixes to spacing of cell mean output.
%
% bugfix 2/6/10 JD
% Fixed bug in code for follow-up ANOVAs that could cause crashes or the wrong factors to be used (but still correctly
% labeled).
%
% bugfix 10/23/14 JD
% Fixed crash when running ANOVA with only between-group factors.
%
% modified 9/20/15 JD
% Added effect sizes.
%
% modified 12/11/15 JD
% Checks if p-value variability exceeds two standard deviations over the threshold.
% Includes warning summary of %age of singular and nearly singular matrices in the output.
%
% modified 8/8/18 JD
% Added follow-up pairwise contrasts for significant effects when the factor has more than two levels.
% Added ANOVA header information to the output.
% Improved output formatting.
%
% modified 8/21/18 JD
% Changed contrast vectors to be Helmert orthogonal sets.
% Now includes MSE and standard error in the output.
% Massively cleaned up the contrast generation code and removed the six factor limitation.
%
% bugfix & modified 10/16/18 JD
% Fixed sometimes skipping follow-up statistics, perhaps due to a change in Matlab?
% Added mex files to speed up ANOVAs.
% Fixed crash for follow-up pairwise contrasts for significant effects when the factor has more than two levels.
%
% bugfix 6/25/19 JD
% Restored compiled functions for robust ANOVAs (oops).
%
% bugfix 10/31/19 JD
% Fixed crash when performing pairwise followups on significant effects with more than two levels and there were also between group factors.
%
% bugfix 11/10/19 JD
% Fixed crash when performing pairwise followups on significant effects with more than two between group levels.
%
% bugfix 12/16/19 JD
% Fixed outputting an extra column of NaNs in the cell mean tables for ANOVAs with no within factors.
%
% modified 12/29/19 JD
% Added support for session ANOVA factors and ICC reliability output.
%
% modified 8/31/20 JD
% Added support for option to run all posthocs even if the effect is not significant.
%
% modified 10/9/20 JD
% NaN values now treated as missing data and MISSING set to Inf if unspecified.
%
% modified & bugfix 1/18/21 JD
% Added option to use conventional ANOVA statistics.
% Loftus-Masson standard errors no longer printed for purely between effects.  Instead, cell standard errors provided.
% Appropriate MCP correction now applied to post-hocs, rather than the factorwise Bonferroni specified in the ANOVA pane.
% Added ANCOVA option to conventional ANOVA statistics.
% Added effect size and statistical power information to conventional ANOVA statistics.
%
% modified 9/24/21 JD
% Added code to detect weird Matlab condition that truncates the statistics lines in the html output files and aborts the run so the user can rerun it again.
%
% modified 10/6/21 JD
% Standard errors based are now provided along with the cell means for within-subject analyses in the ANOVA outputs.
%
% bugfix 11/16/21 JD
% Fixed standard errors printing out as NaN when there are missing values present.
%
% bugfix 5/13/22 JD
% Fixed not providing separate follow-ups for between-group factor levels.
%
% bugfix 6/3/22 JD
% Fixed crashing when there are more than two between-group factors.
%
% bugfix 8/14/22 JD
% Fixed standard errors only going out to two decimals and thus often being zero for accuracy data.
%
% bugfix 7/31/23 JD
% Fixed standard errors only going out to two decimals and thus often being zero for accuracy data.
%
% bugfix & modified 8/20/24 JD
% Fixed Winsorized Standard Errors in output cell tables computed incorrectly.  Test statistics not affected.
% Fixed robust MSE computed incorrectly for designs with multiple factors.  Test statistics not affected.
% Changed vector scaling test to compute scaling within collapsed cells rather than original cells.  Test statistics potentially affected.
% Added parallel computing calls to accelerate robust statistics.
% Added buttons to ANOVA output html for selecting between cell means tables with SD, Cousineau-Moray SE, robust Cousineau-Moray CI, outlier plots, or plots with CI error bars.
% Changed output of trimmed means to be those of collapsed cells (e.g., with 5% trimming, two separate cells with n=10 each would not be trimmed whereas
%    a collapsed cell with n=20 would be trimmed.  Means tables and test statistics potentially affected.
% Added mean R to output for use with power calculations.
% Added d<sub>R</sub> effect sizes and confidence intervals for pairwise within-group effects as well plus effect sizes for between and within group effects with more than two levels.
% Added bold for non-significant effects with confidence interval boundaries less than absolute .2.
%
% bugfix 9/7/24 JD
% Fixed crash when doing vector scaling test with collapsed cells.
%
% bugfix 10/3/24 JD
% Fixed crash in some cases when doing followup within contrasts.
%
% bugfix & modified 11/15/24 JD
% Fixed error bars in ANOVA figures too large.
% Improved scaling of ANOVA bar plots.
%
% bugfix 3/27/25 JD
% Fixed crash in some cases due to bug in new improved scaling of ANOVA bar plots.
% Fixed outlier plots for between group effects displaying same cell for all levels.
% Added support for virtual grand averages.
% Fixed crash when running ANOVAs with a between factor and a within electrode factor.
% Added final pairwise contrasts between all cells.
%
% modified 5/28/25 JD
% Added EP version and time to statistics header output.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
